 Deployment helpers for the internal services.
 
-`bin/notify-deploy.sh SERVICE VERSION` restarts the service on the deploy host and posts a
-message to the release channel. The webhook URL comes from `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` in the
+`bin/notify-deploy.sh [--dry-run] SERVICE VERSION [NOTE]` restarts the service on the deploy host
+and posts a message to the release channel. The webhook URL comes from `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` in the
 environment; per-service settings live in `etc/services.conf`.
+
+## Release note
+
+An optional third argument is a short release note. It is added on a new line below the
+"Deployed SERVICE VERSION by USER" message in Slack:
+
+    SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=... bin/notify-deploy.sh web 1.4.2 "Fixes the login timeout"
+
+The note is at most 500 characters and may not contain control characters (such as newlines).
+Quote it so the shell passes it as a single argument.
+
+## Dry run
+
+`--dry-run` (accepted anywhere on the command line) prints what the script would do and exits
+without restarting the service or posting to Slack:
+
+    bin/notify-deploy.sh --dry-run web 1.4.2 "Fixes the login timeout"
+
+It shows the `ssh` command and the Slack payload. The webhook URL is never printed; the output
+only says whether `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` is set. `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` is only required for a real run.
+
+## Input rules
+
+`SERVICE` must match `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*` (up to 64 characters) and `VERSION` must start with a
+letter or digit and contain only letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `+` and `-` (up to 64 characters).
+Anything else is rejected with exit status 65.
